MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Wires Phase 7's LutCodec + frame-CRC routines into a new stage2 mode
that ingests a base .rbf + edit list from SD, applies LUT-mask edits
in SDRAM, and recomputes frame CRCs over the dirty set.  Halts before
EPCS write (Phases 2–4 pending hardware).

Format
------

sd_layout.py bumps to LAYOUT_VERSION=4: header gains edits_lba +
edits_sz in the previously-reserved [56..64) slot, no hash-slot
reflow.  New SD region at LBA 8729 (32 KB cap) holds edits.bin.

edits.bin is a 16-byte header + packed 8-byte records:
  magic     0x45444954 ('EDIT' big-endian on wire)
  version   1
  n_records u32
  crc32     CRC-32/ISO-HDLC over [version | n_records | records]
  edit_rec  (x:u16, y:u16, n:u16, mask:u16) × n_records

The magic is excluded from the CRC payload (per plan v3 §Phase 8) so
a 4-byte partial read can't pass the gate by luck.

Integrity gate, not authentication
----------------------------------

mode_g re-checks magic + version + CRC32 before applying any record.
A bit-flip in the records body produces a recomputed CRC that won't
match the header field → halt before any EPCS touch.  CRC32 detects
accidental SD-read corruption; an attacker who can rewrite edits.bin
can also rewrite the CRC.  Authentication remains upstream (signed by
the user's NN compiler); a future Ed25519-on-edits.bin gate would
land as a Phase 8 hardening row, mirroring Phase 7's "Anti-pattern to
reject" note.

This closes a hole the plan calls out specifically: without the gate,
lc_patch_rbf_crc_frames legitimises corrupted edits by recomputing
correct frame CRCs over the corrupted CRAM data, producing a
well-formed-but-wrong bitstream.

mode_g flow (main.c)
--------------------

  1. Read base.rbf from h->tpu_lba into TPU_SCRATCH_ADDR (mode_t reuse).
  2. Read edits.bin from h->edits_lba into EDITS_SCRATCH_ADDR
     (0x41E00000, 1 MB below DTB).
  2a. Magic + version + CRC32 gate.
  3. SHA-256 base.rbf vs rot_golden_tpu_bitstream (the canonical base).
  5. For each record: lut_apply_with_canon + dirty bitmap (lc_init
     whitelist rejects out-of-pin-friendly tuples).
  6. lc_patch_rbf_crc_frames over the dirty set.
  7. SHA-256 of the result, print to UART (no golden compare —
     output is design-time-unknown).
  8-10. EPCS write + read-back verify + ALTREMOTE_UPDATE reconfig:
     deferred until Phases 2–4 land.  Halts here with the same gate
     mode_t uses.

CRC32 refactor
--------------

Extracted crc32_update / crc32 / crc32_two from main.c into a new
crc32_iso.{c,h} TU so host/test_edits_bin.py can compile + ctypes-
link the exact code stage2 runs.  This gives Phase 8 a CI-time gate
on host↔chip CRC equivalence — analogous to how Phase 7's
test_lutcodec_c.py validates the lutcodec port.  The xmodem upload
path's CRC reuses the same routine (it was already CRC-32/ISO-HDLC).

Host-side
---------

  host/gen_edits.py     emits edits.bin from JSON or CLI tuples;
                        whitelist-validates against the same
                        PIN_X × LAB_Y_CE6 × even-N set lc_init
                        enforces on chip.
  host/sd_pack.py       grows --edits PATH; auto-discovers
                        output/edits.bin if present.
  host/test_edits_bin.py round-trip + CRC cross-test (host vs C)
                         + 5 negative cases + 5 whitelist rejections.

Memory budget
-------------

stage2 ELF: 9492 → 13908 B.  +4416 B for mode_g + lutcodec/crc16
linkage (previously dead-stripped) + crc32_iso TU.  IMEM 32 K
has ~18 K slack.  bss +728 B (mode_g_dirty 217 B + alignment +
xmodem buf).  Far below the M9K budget.

Validation
----------

  $ cd sw/stage2_loader && make all              # 13908 B
  $ EP4CE6_REPO=/home/test/EP4CE6 \
      python3 host/test_lutcodec_c.py            # 127/127 still
  $ python3 host/test_edits_bin.py               # 5/5 paths green
  $ python3 -c 'import sd_layout as L; \
      L.verify_layout(L.parse_header( \
      L.build_header(0,0,0,edits_sz=128)))'      # v4 round-trip

End-to-end silicon test deferred to Phase 6.  bench needs an AX301
running a base bitstream whose LUTs are pinned to the pin-friendly
column subset — the same constraint Phase 7's LutCodec port assumes.

